1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:05,860
How can choosing equipment and appearance become an enjoyable part of a game?

2
00:00:06,280 --> 00:00:10,950
We will explore designs that help players understand a choice, try it, and

3
00:00:10,950 --> 00:00:12,780
express a preferred identity.

4
00:00:12,958 --> 00:00:16,964
We will read visible effects in Warframe's Gauss and Jade demonstrations, then

5
00:00:16,964 --> 00:00:20,458
move through configuration choices, trials, and appearance previews.

6
00:00:20,758 --> 00:00:24,057
First, watch the path Gauss travels and the enemies' reactions.

7
00:00:24,083 --> 00:00:28,558
In the official Gauss ability demonstration, the character dashes forward and

8
00:00:28,558 --> 00:00:30,243
enemies along the path react.

9
00:00:30,583 --> 00:00:34,495
Another shot shows a circular effect spreading across the ground. We can

10
00:00:34,495 --> 00:00:37,103
distinguish a travel path from an affected area.

11
00:00:37,116 --> 00:00:41,066
Equipment choices should support this kind of prediction. Beside a number

12
00:00:41,066 --> 00:00:44,096
describing increased range, show the space it can reach.

13
00:00:44,616 --> 00:00:48,366
Help players understand which actions a change enables, instead of teaching

14
00:00:48,366 --> 00:00:50,616
them to select the largest number every time.

15
00:00:50,883 --> 00:00:55,617
As Gauss moves, follow the starting position and the line he travels. Watching

16
00:00:55,617 --> 00:01:00,289
where the enemies stood and how they react afterward helps connect the effect

17
00:01:00,289 --> 00:01:01,563
to the movement path.

18
00:01:01,843 --> 00:01:05,771
Next, watch the attack motion and the effect spreading across the floor. The

19
00:01:05,771 --> 00:01:09,441
direction the character faces is different from the space around him. A

20
00:01:09,441 --> 00:01:12,903
selection screen should make clear which of these a choice changes.

21
00:01:12,933 --> 00:01:16,728
In Dante's developer demonstration, glowing projectiles travel toward the

22
00:01:16,728 --> 00:01:20,678
direction he aims. Follow the character's position, the direction of travel,

23
00:01:20,678 --> 00:01:22,653
and the visible target at the far end.

24
00:01:22,933 --> 00:01:26,979
Calling both actions attacks does little to explain the difference. Show

25
00:01:26,979 --> 00:01:31,250
whether a choice changes the space crossed while moving or an effect sent in

26
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:34,453
one direction, and players will know what action to test.

27
00:01:34,483 --> 00:01:39,049
When Dante raises his arms or aims ahead, look for where the bright effect

28
00:01:39,049 --> 00:01:43,799
first appears. Finding the motion near his hands and body makes the direction

29
00:01:43,799 --> 00:01:46,143
of the following light easier to read.

30
00:01:46,323 --> 00:01:50,450
For the projectile shots, watch the space between the caster and the target

31
00:01:50,450 --> 00:01:54,687
ahead. Beyond the color of the effect, trace where it begins, which direction

32
00:01:54,687 --> 00:01:56,943
it takes, and where it appears to arrive.

33
00:01:57,173 --> 00:02:01,541
Another shot shows a round blue effect near the caster. Compare the forward

34
00:02:01,541 --> 00:02:06,083
line with the round shape remaining nearby. These shots ask viewers to inspect

35
00:02:06,083 --> 00:02:07,073
different spaces.

36
00:02:07,353 --> 00:02:11,398
You can build that connection into your own preview. Instead of only swapping

37
00:02:11,398 --> 00:02:15,390
a tool icon, reveal the action's origin, the space it covers, and the target

38
00:02:15,390 --> 00:02:17,333
used to check its effect in sequence.

39
00:02:17,367 --> 00:02:21,439
In the Jade developer demonstration, an ability is followed by ground effects,

40
00:02:21,439 --> 00:02:23,579
marks above enemies, and enemy reactions.

41
00:02:23,887 --> 00:02:27,487
Follow the connection between where the action starts and which targets it

42
00:02:27,487 --> 00:02:29,287
affects, rather than a single number.

43
00:02:29,557 --> 00:02:34,438
An explanation should move in the same order: reveal the selected tool, extend

44
00:02:34,438 --> 00:02:37,317
its reach, and then show the targets changing.

45
00:02:37,597 --> 00:02:41,534
Players should be able to predict a result before choosing and recognize it

46
00:02:41,534 --> 00:02:45,419
after using the choice. This shot alone cannot establish that a particular

47
00:02:45,419 --> 00:02:47,257
configuration improved performance.

48
00:02:47,300 --> 00:02:51,386
Look more closely at Jade creating an effect on the ground. Identify the

49
00:02:51,386 --> 00:02:55,699
acting character, the light on the floor, and the nearby enemies separately,

50
00:02:55,699 --> 00:02:58,480
then connect the changes occurring in that space.

51
00:02:58,760 --> 00:03:03,431
Now the character is airborne and aims downward. Unlike the ground-level shot,

52
00:03:03,431 --> 00:03:08,043
there is a difference in height. Watch the character's position together with

53
00:03:08,043 --> 00:03:09,600
the targets visible below.

54
00:03:09,630 --> 00:03:14,171
The higher view helps distinguish targets on the ground from the aiming

55
00:03:14,171 --> 00:03:19,032
direction. The motion alone does not demonstrate that a particular equipment

56
00:03:19,032 --> 00:03:21,270
setting increased height or damage.

57
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:25,411
The viewpoint matters when explaining a choice's outcome. Keep the spatial

58
00:03:25,411 --> 00:03:29,490
relationship players can actually check, such as reaching along the ground or

59
00:03:29,490 --> 00:03:32,510
aiming down from above, in both the preview and the test.

60
00:03:32,583 --> 00:03:36,418
A straight travel path and an effect spreading around a character occupy

61
00:03:36,418 --> 00:03:37,323
different spaces.

62
00:03:37,623 --> 00:03:41,521
The Yareli demonstration shows a rotating effect around the moving character.

63
00:03:41,521 --> 00:03:44,103
Watch the spatial relationship with nearby targets.

64
00:03:44,333 --> 00:03:48,631
These differences can become reasons to choose. A tool for clustered targets

65
00:03:48,631 --> 00:03:52,533
can have different strengths from one for a distant, isolated target.

66
00:03:52,813 --> 00:03:56,400
If one option is better in every situation, the others can become decoration.

67
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:00,034
Explain the situation where a strength matters and the opportunity that option

68
00:04:00,034 --> 00:04:00,453
gives up.

69
00:04:00,583 --> 00:04:04,813
Yareli's rotating effect moves with the character. Watch the character at its

70
00:04:04,813 --> 00:04:08,933
center together with nearby targets, rather than only following the circle.

71
00:04:08,933 --> 00:04:12,503
Their relative positions change the space that appears reachable.

72
00:04:12,683 --> 00:04:16,749
Movement can carry you past a target or around its side. To explain a

73
00:04:16,749 --> 00:04:21,227
surrounding effect, a static size diagram benefits from showing the relative

74
00:04:21,227 --> 00:04:23,643
positions during actual movement as well.

75
00:04:23,953 --> 00:04:28,785
Watch Dante's round effect with the same approach. Distinguish its visible

76
00:04:28,785 --> 00:04:33,485
center from the targets ahead. Similar shapes do not establish identical

77
00:04:33,485 --> 00:04:35,313
ability functions or values.

78
00:04:35,873 --> 00:04:40,648
These shots do not supply an answer about the best ability. They offer a way

79
00:04:40,648 --> 00:04:45,359
to show players interested in nearby space where to look and what action to

80
00:04:45,359 --> 00:04:45,673
test.

81
00:04:45,717 --> 00:04:50,271
When Dante aims toward the doorway, first inspect the door and the visible

82
00:04:50,271 --> 00:04:54,947
targets in front of it. Follow the bright effect's direction as well to read

83
00:04:54,947 --> 00:04:56,917
which space he is acting toward.

84
00:04:57,137 --> 00:05:01,779
Aiming from another position changes the angle between caster and target. A

85
00:05:01,779 --> 00:05:06,050
target centered on screen is not necessarily directly in front of the

86
00:05:06,050 --> 00:05:08,897
character. Consider the camera's position too.

87
00:05:08,909 --> 00:05:12,801
In your own selection system, the same candidate could be tested against

88
00:05:12,801 --> 00:05:17,018
different arrangements. Prepare situations matching the player's purpose, such

89
00:05:17,018 --> 00:05:19,829
as targets grouped nearby and a target farther away.

90
00:05:20,029 --> 00:05:24,510
The demonstration illustrates these spatial relationships. Instead of ranking

91
00:05:24,510 --> 00:05:28,700
performance because an effect looks larger in another shot, decide which

92
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:31,609
conditions your own comparison will hold constant.

93
00:05:31,650 --> 00:05:35,545
The configuration screen contains several tabs, cards, and related values.

94
00:05:35,545 --> 00:05:38,650
More options also require a designed path for finding them.

95
00:05:38,890 --> 00:05:42,722
Group choices by role, distinguish them with different icon shapes, and place

96
00:05:42,722 --> 00:05:45,410
a candidate near the current selection for comparison.

97
00:05:45,440 --> 00:05:49,227
Rather than requiring players to read every item immediately, show a useful

98
00:05:49,227 --> 00:05:51,600
starting point and the parts that would change.

99
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:56,290
A recommendation need not claim to be the best answer forever. Leave a clear

100
00:05:56,290 --> 00:05:59,540
route toward candidates that serve a different purpose.

101
00:05:59,583 --> 00:06:04,479
Before entering a selection screen, describe the action you want to try in one

102
00:06:04,479 --> 00:06:09,312
sentence. Watching Dante, you might distinguish aiming at a target ahead from

103
00:06:09,312 --> 00:06:11,383
inspecting space near the caster.

104
00:06:11,723 --> 00:06:16,613
Watch the arm-raising cast and the forward projectile motion in turn.

105
00:06:16,613 --> 00:06:21,645
Imagining the action that will change gives you a starting point before

106
00:06:21,645 --> 00:06:23,983
memorizing every name and number.

107
00:06:24,193 --> 00:06:28,341
Then inspect the targets ahead and the space nearby. These shots do not

108
00:06:28,341 --> 00:06:32,839
contain a complete before-and-after settings change, so they cannot establish

109
00:06:32,839 --> 00:06:35,993
that applying a particular setting altered the effect.

110
00:06:36,253 --> 00:06:40,343
In your own game, show the changing action beside a purpose-based starting

111
00:06:40,343 --> 00:06:44,489
choice. Lead the selected candidate into a short test of that purpose while

112
00:06:44,489 --> 00:06:47,473
preserving a path back to compare the original choice.

113
00:06:47,517 --> 00:06:52,634
As Jade approaches a downed ally, the distance decreases, revive progress

114
00:06:52,634 --> 00:06:54,877
rises, and the ally moves again.

115
00:06:55,177 --> 00:06:59,166
A trial should let players perform their intended task. A range choice can be

116
00:06:59,166 --> 00:07:03,000
tested against reachable targets; a support choice can be tested through a

117
00:07:03,000 --> 00:07:03,777
helping action.

118
00:07:03,797 --> 00:07:07,664
Long travel and repeated trips through complex screens can discourage trying

119
00:07:07,664 --> 00:07:10,717
another combination, even when players have an idea for one.

120
00:07:10,857 --> 00:07:14,721
Connect choosing, a short trial, and returning to the choice. Keeping the

121
00:07:14,721 --> 00:07:18,057
previous selection makes comparison and experimentation easier.

122
00:07:18,083 --> 00:07:22,736
As Jade approaches a fallen ally, the intended recipient of help is clear.

123
00:07:22,736 --> 00:07:27,640
Watching the approach and subsequent recovery together explains what this test

124
00:07:27,640 --> 00:07:29,023
is trying to complete.

125
00:07:29,203 --> 00:07:33,898
An attack test asks you to observe something different. When Dante aims ahead

126
00:07:33,898 --> 00:07:38,653
and sends out an effect, follow the initial motion and the target in front. It

127
00:07:38,653 --> 00:07:41,823
need not use the support shot's completion criteria.

128
00:07:41,846 --> 00:07:46,593
Use the two shots to separate your own test goals. Provide someone to help for

129
00:07:46,593 --> 00:07:51,158
a support choice, or a position to check when changing reach. Watching only

130
00:07:51,158 --> 00:07:54,566
how spectacular an effect looks can obscure the purpose.

131
00:07:54,806 --> 00:07:59,167
After the test, carry what was checked back into the selection screen.

132
00:07:59,167 --> 00:08:03,654
Returning to a candidate while remembering the observed result helps the

133
00:08:03,654 --> 00:08:06,146
player decide which part to change next.

134
00:08:06,417 --> 00:08:11,508
Connect the real action shots we have examined. Moving forward along a path

135
00:08:11,508 --> 00:08:16,802
and sending an effect toward a target suggest different actions a player might

136
00:08:16,802 --> 00:08:17,617
want to try.

137
00:08:17,997 --> 00:08:22,918
An effect near the caster or aiming downward from above changes the positions

138
00:08:22,918 --> 00:08:27,647
to observe. These differences can help explain selection outcomes and test

139
00:08:27,647 --> 00:08:29,437
situations in your own game.

140
00:08:29,467 --> 00:08:33,593
The color and appearance previews coming next ask another question. Alongside

141
00:08:33,593 --> 00:08:37,772
which targets to affect, they ask what look to create. Appearance alone should

142
00:08:37,772 --> 00:08:40,827
not determine judgments about those actions' performance.

143
00:08:41,087 --> 00:08:45,424
Prepare both a test for understanding an action and a preview for checking a

144
00:08:45,424 --> 00:08:49,818
desired look. Distinguishing the two helps players know what will change. Now

145
00:08:49,818 --> 00:08:51,987
we can move on to personal expression.

146
00:08:52,017 --> 00:08:55,924
Color and appearance previews show a possible result before a separate

147
00:08:55,924 --> 00:08:57,097
decision to apply it.

148
00:08:57,397 --> 00:09:01,678
Creating a preferred look can be valuable even without a performance change. A

149
00:09:01,678 --> 00:09:04,697
striking look and a cute look express different tastes.

150
00:09:04,724 --> 00:09:09,295
Make previewed and saved states distinguishable. Show what would change, how

151
00:09:09,295 --> 00:09:12,904
to confirm it, and how to return to the previous appearance.

152
00:09:13,084 --> 00:09:17,409
Do not describe a cosmetic choice as evidence of increased power. Consider the

153
00:09:17,409 --> 00:09:21,124
enjoyment offered by play styles and appearance on their own terms.

154
00:09:21,150 --> 00:09:25,499
Customization can create enjoyment in choosing, trying, and changing, as well

155
00:09:25,499 --> 00:09:27,250
as in the finished combination.

156
00:09:27,530 --> 00:09:32,005
Pick one choice in your game. Can players predict its result, find a reason to

157
00:09:32,005 --> 00:09:36,308
choose another option in a different situation, and try it before returning

158
00:09:36,308 --> 00:09:36,710
easily?

159
00:09:36,740 --> 00:09:41,125
Finally, let them build a preferred appearance and clearly confirm the choice.

160
00:09:41,125 --> 00:09:44,780
This small process connects imagination to action and expression.

161
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:48,296
If you want to implement and evaluate a choice flow yourself, find the

162
00:09:48,296 --> 00:09:50,640
programming coaching information in the description.
